m i  i found a lot of my sam in those  interrogation scenes in that hotel room  that that taught me who he was  fighting for something you you know what  i mean  i mean having to defend myself and and  having  kingsley as malcolm pushed me around a  bit and  you know questioned me it it made me  stronger  and so the day that we shot that  that scene i just sam was never closer  to me than he was on that day  uh and it completely shows on screen um  the movie takes place in 1964  but it's as relevant today as it was  back then  and i'm curious do you feel like  over the last year with all the things  that have happened with black lives  matter and just everything that's been  bubbling up do you feel like  like that maybe the country and the  world is on the precipice  of a change or do you still think it's  going to be um  you know decades of a fight and it's  still going to be a fight  all the time oh i i  i i am hopeful guy that's what gets me  out of bed i'm hopeful  and uh i i i believe that it's  it's closer than ever that's the kind of  guy than i am  enlightenment um an awakening  um yeah change is is  always available to us and um we have  another opportunity right now  to uh to to  draw closer to to the  our better angels and make the right  choices you know and and heal  old wounds you know but there's there's  ghosts  here in america you know talking about  the the history of native americans in  this country certainly the history of  slavery  in this country there's lot that we have  yet really to reckon with  and maybe we can do it once and for all  i don't know  one of the things that when i speak to a  lot of actors who are portraying a real  person  in a in a serious role like this a lot  of times uh people stay in character  even when they're you know all the time  just to or they keep a voice or they do  something to stay in that headspace  did you do that while playing sam at all  or were you able to just  sort of shut him off and turn yourself  back on no i  i do it for a time so i did it probably  for about three or four weeks  you know but that's intense it is it's  exhausting too  you know to to keep it all up in the  breaks and stuff  too um to keep up the dialect to keep up  that that pitch you know because sam's  voice was higher than mine it's it's  tiring  uh but it's necessary to really sort of  to lock it in  you want it locked in but by the by the  time we were done shooting i was able to  go  in and out but for the first few weeks  it is really it is helpful  to to stay in it uh i am a big fan of  central park  and uh i thought you did i thought you  did such a great job with that and i  love the songs i i'm really a huge fan  of the series  i know you're recording you're doing  season two right now yeah  what can you tease people about uh some  of the songs and  the the story i record it right here  right like literally right  this is where i record central park um  it's  just just i was saying to my wife the  other day i finished i think  episode i don't know maybe the third or  fourth episode of the second season and  i was just telling her  how much better it is my favorite  animated shows if you go back to  you know the long-running ones you go  back to the first season they're almost  unrecognizable it takes a while in  animation to really  find your thing to gel as a cast you're  recording all separately  it takes a while for the writers to hone  in on the character to hone in on your  strengths as an actor so  uh i'm pleasantly i'm just looking  forward to  you guys getting a glimpse at season two  because i think it's even better  love it i love hearing this listen i  already gotta go i'm just gonna say  congrats on everything  and thank you so much for your time and  being you know congrats on being part of  this movie  thanks brother be well

